---
/**
 * Ambient chess-piece silhouettes behind the home hero.
 *
 * Decoration, and treated as such: `aria-hidden`, `pointer-events: none`, and
 * nothing here carries meaning that is not also in the text.
 *
 * ── Why these shapes are hand-drawn ──────────────────────────────────────
 * The cburnett piece set that ships with Chessground is **CC BY-SA 3.0**. Using
 * it here would drag an attribution-and-share-alike obligation onto ordinary
 * page decoration, and `/mentions-legales/` credits it specifically as the piece
 * set *on the board*. These are original geometric silhouettes — plain shapes,
 * no derivation — so they carry no obligation at all.
 *
 * ── Why there is no JavaScript ───────────────────────────────────────────
 * The drift is CSS keyframes and the parallax is a CSS scroll-driven animation
 * behind `@supports`. Where `animation-timeline` is unsupported the pieces still
 * drift and simply do not parallax, which is a degradation nobody can perceive
 * without a side-by-side. That is worth more than a scroll listener on the
 * critical path of the site's most-visited page.
 *
 * ══ TWO LAYERS, AND WHY (E1) ═════════════════════════════════════════════
 * There is a NEAR layer and a FAR layer, and the difference between them is the
 * whole effect. A single layer of drifting shapes is texture; two layers moving
 * at different rates is depth, and depth is what makes a flat cream page read
 * as a room.
 *
 * Depth comes from the RATE, not the period. The far pieces travel about a
 * third as far as the near ones over a longer cycle, so they move perhaps four
 * times slower in pixels per second — which is what the eye reads as distance.
 * Giving them a longer period alone would just have made them lazier, not
 * further away.
 *
 * ⚠️ THE OPACITY CEILING IS 0.075 AND IS ENFORCED BY THE GROUP, NOT BY EACH
 * PIECE. `.ambient` carries `--mcc-ambient-opacity`, and group opacity is
 * applied to the flattened group — so two overlapping pieces composite to the
 * group's alpha and NOT to the sum of their own. That is the only reason a
 * second layer could be added at all without re-auditing the hero text against
 * a new worst case. Do not move the opacity down onto the pieces.
 *
 * ⚠️ DRIFT PERIODS LIVE IN THE AMBIANCE BAND: 4–20s (see `src/lib/motion.ts`).
 * They were 47–71s before E1, which is slow enough that a reader landing on the
 * page sees no motion at all in their first five seconds — the layer was paying
 * its full cost and delivering nothing. The periods below are mutually
 * non-multiple so the arrangement never visibly repeats.
 */
---

<div class="ambient" aria-hidden="true">
  {/* ── NEAR: larger, higher contrast, travels furthest ─────────────────── */}
  <div class="layer layer-near">
    <svg class="piece piece-pawn" viewBox="0 0 40 64" fill="currentColor">
      <circle cx="20" cy="12" r="8"></circle>
      <path d="M12 21h16l-2.5 6.5C29.5 34 31 42 31 51H9c0-9 1.5-17 5.5-23.5z"></path>
      <rect x="5" y="53" width="30" height="8" rx="3"></rect>
    </svg>

    <svg class="piece piece-rook" viewBox="0 0 44 64" fill="currentColor">
      <path d="M6 5h7v6h6V5h6v6h6V5h7v15l-4 4v20l4 6v8H6v-8l4-6V24l-4-4z"></path>
    </svg>

    <svg class="piece piece-bishop" viewBox="0 0 44 64" fill="currentColor">
      <circle cx="22" cy="7" r="4.5"></circle>
      <path d="M22 12c8.5 6.5 13 15 13 23 0 6.5-5.5 11-13 11S9 41.5 9 35c0-8 4.5-16.5 13-23z"
      ></path>
      <rect x="7" y="49" width="30" height="8" rx="3"></rect>
    </svg>

    <svg class="piece piece-king" viewBox="0 0 44 64" fill="currentColor">
      <path d="M19.5 3h5v4h4v5h-4v5h-5v-5h-4V7h4z"></path>
      <path d="M22 18c9 5 14 13 14 21 0 6.5-6 11-14 11S8 45.5 8 39c0-8 5-16 14-21z"></path>
      <rect x="6" y="53" width="32" height="8" rx="3"></rect>
    </svg>
  </div>

  {/* ── FAR: fainter, slower, barely travels. The room behind the room. ─── */}
  <div class="layer layer-far">
    <svg class="piece piece-queen" viewBox="0 0 48 64" fill="currentColor">
      <path d="M5 12l5 13 6-17 8 17 8-17 6 17 5-13 3 26c-4 4-11 6-22 6S6 42 6 38z"></path>
      <rect x="7" y="46" width="34" height="9" rx="3"></rect>
    </svg>

    <svg class="piece piece-knight" viewBox="0 0 44 64" fill="currentColor">
      <path
        d="M15 5c8-2 16 2 20 10 3 7 3 16 0 23-2 5-6 9-11 12h9v6H9v-6c0-7 4-13 10-17l-9-3-3-10 8-5z"
      ></path>
    </svg>

    <svg class="piece piece-pawn piece-pawn-far" viewBox="0 0 40 64" fill="currentColor">
      <circle cx="20" cy="12" r="8"></circle>
      <path d="M12 21h16l-2.5 6.5C29.5 34 31 42 31 51H9c0-9 1.5-17 5.5-23.5z"></path>
      <rect x="5" y="53" width="30" height="8" rx="3"></rect>
    </svg>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .ambient {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* Behind the hero's own text, which sets no z-index of its own. */
    z-index: 0;
    /* THE CEILING. The hero text must stay readable over this at every
       viewport, so the whole layer is capped here rather than trusting each
       piece — and, since E1, rather than trusting each LAYER. Group opacity
       flattens first, so overlapping pieces from the two layers can never
       composite past this value. See the header note. */
    opacity: var(--mcc-ambient-opacity);
    color: var(--mcc-ambient);
  }

  .layer {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  /* The far layer's share of the ceiling. Held as a token because the two
     palettes want different amounts of it: light mode starts flatter (0.055 vs
     0.07), so it takes a bigger share of a smaller budget and gains the most
     from the second layer — which is exactly what E1 asked for. */
  .layer-far {
    opacity: var(--mcc-ambient-far);
  }

  .piece {
    position: absolute;
    width: auto;
    /* Deliberately large and soft: at this opacity a small crisp piece reads as
       a stray glyph, where a large one reads as panelling. */
    height: clamp(9rem, 26vw, 18rem);
    animation: drift linear infinite;
  }

  /* Promoted only on the near layer. The far pieces animate a transform too, so
     the compositor promotes them anyway when the animation starts; `will-change`
     buys a smoother first frame and costs a retained layer, and seven retained
     layers of full-bleed SVG is not a trade worth making on a phone. */
  .layer-near .piece {
    will-change: transform;
  }

  /* ── NEAR: four rhythms, all inside the 4–20s Ambiance band, mutually
     non-multiple so the arrangement does not visibly repeat. ───────────── */
  .piece-pawn {
    top: 4%;
    left: 4%;
    animation-duration: 13s;
  }

  .piece-rook {
    top: 30%;
    right: 6%;
    height: clamp(7rem, 20vw, 14rem);
    animation-duration: 19s;
    animation-delay: -12s;
  }

  .piece-bishop {
    bottom: -6%;
    left: 26%;
    height: clamp(8rem, 22vw, 15rem);
    animation-duration: 15s;
    animation-delay: -30s;
  }

  .piece-king {
    top: 12%;
    right: 32%;
    height: clamp(6rem, 16vw, 11rem);
    animation-duration: 17s;
    animation-delay: -5s;
  }

  /* ── FAR: bigger, fainter, and travelling a third as far. ─────────────── */
  .layer-far .piece {
    animation-name: drift-far;
  }

  .piece-queen {
    top: -4%;
    left: 44%;
    height: clamp(11rem, 32vw, 22rem);
    animation-duration: 16s;
    animation-delay: -9s;
  }

  .piece-knight {
    bottom: 2%;
    right: 18%;
    height: clamp(10rem, 28vw, 19rem);
    animation-duration: 20s;
    animation-delay: -3s;
  }

  .piece-pawn-far {
    top: 44%;
    left: 12%;
    height: clamp(8rem, 24vw, 16rem);
    animation-duration: 18s;
    animation-delay: -14s;
  }

  @keyframes drift {
    0% {
      transform: translate3d(0, 0, 0) rotate(-3deg);
    }
    50% {
      transform: translate3d(1.5rem, -2rem, 0) rotate(3deg);
    }
    100% {
      transform: translate3d(0, 0, 0) rotate(-3deg);
    }
  }

  /* A third of the travel over a longer cycle. That ratio IS the depth. */
  @keyframes drift-far {
    0% {
      transform: translate3d(0, 0, 0) rotate(-1deg);
    }
    50% {
      transform: translate3d(0.5rem, -0.7rem, 0) rotate(1deg);
    }
    100% {
      transform: translate3d(0, 0, 0) rotate(-1deg);
    }
  }

  /* ── Parallax, CSS-only ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     `animation-timeline: scroll()` drives this from the scroll position with
     no listener and no main-thread work. Gated on @supports: where it is
     unavailable the pieces keep drifting and simply do not parallax.

     Each layer moves a different distance — that difference IS the parallax;
     a uniform shift would just be the page scrolling. The far pieces shift
     least, for the same reason they drift least. */
  @supports (animation-timeline: scroll()) {
    .piece {
      animation:
        drift linear infinite,
        parallax linear;
      animation-timeline: auto, scroll();
    }

    .layer-far .piece {
      animation-name: drift-far, parallax;
    }

    .piece-pawn {
      --shift: -4rem;
    }
    .piece-rook {
      --shift: -8rem;
    }
    .piece-bishop {
      --shift: -2.5rem;
    }
    .piece-king {
      --shift: -6rem;
    }

    .piece-queen {
      --shift: -1.5rem;
    }
    .piece-knight {
      --shift: -2rem;
    }
    .piece-pawn-far {
      --shift: -1rem;
    }

    @keyframes parallax {
      to {
        translate: 0 var(--shift, -4rem);
      }
    }
  }

  /* ── Reduced motion ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The layers stay — they are texture, and removing them would change the
     page's composition rather than just its motion — but nothing moves.

     OFF, not slower. Ambiance is the one family with no reduced-motion
     duration at all: there is no version of decorative drift that a reader who
     asked for stillness wants at a different speed. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    /* ⚠️ BOTH SELECTORS, AND THE SECOND IS NOT REDUNDANT.
       The `@supports` block above sets `animation-name` via `.layer-far .piece`
       — two classes — so a single-class off-switch here loses the specificity
       fight and the far layer keeps drifting. It did, until a spec caught it.
       Anything added to that block needs a matching selector here. */
    .piece,
    .layer-far .piece {
      animation: none;
      translate: none;
    }
  }
</style>
